<meta type=book>
<title | Foundations of Reproducible Research>
<subtitle | A Short Edited Volume>
<editor | Dana Reed>
</meta>


<preface | About This Volume>
Reproducibility is less a technique than a habit: a set of small, unglamorous
practices that, taken together, let someone else — including your future self —
rebuild a result from its inputs. This short volume collects three such habits, one
per chapter, each written by a different contributor.<note | The chapters were
commissioned independently; the editor has imposed only a common notation and the
shared checklist reproduced in the appendix.>

<chapter | Version Control for Scientific Work>
<author | Priya Raman>
A version-control system records the history of a project as a sequence of
*commits*, each a complete snapshot tied to its parent. The result is a directed
graph of states that can be branched, merged, and — crucially for reproducibility —
referred to by an immutable identifier.<note | Distributed systems such as Git give
every clone the full history, so the record does not depend on a central server
staying alive.>

<fig #fig:vcs-graph src="https://picsum.photos/seed/commit-graph/800/400" | A commit graph with two branches merging. Each node is an immutable snapshot; the labels are human-readable names for otherwise opaque commit hashes.>

The discipline that matters is not the tool but the granularity: small, frequent,
well-described commits, as <cite @Wilson2014> argue. <ref @fig:vcs-graph> shows the
shape such a history takes.

<chapter | Literate Programming and Notebooks>
<author | Marcus Feld>
Literate programming, introduced by <cite @Knuth1984>, inverts the usual relation
between code and documentation: the program is written as an essay for human
readers, with the machine-executable fragments extracted from it. Computational
notebooks are its modern, interactive descendant.

The reproducibility payoff is that narrative, code, and output live in one artifact.
The cost is *execution-order fragility*: a notebook's stored output reflects whatever
order its cells were last run in, which need not be top to bottom. A simple guard is
to require that the notebook restart-and-run-all cleanly, so the stored state
satisfies

<$$ #eqn:nb-invariant | \text{stored output} = f(\text{inputs}, \text{cells top to bottom}) $$>

before it is committed.<note | Many notebook platforms can enforce this in
continuous integration: a notebook that fails restart-and-run-all is rejected the
same way a failing test is.>

<fig #fig:nb-pipeline src="https://picsum.photos/seed/notebook-ci/800/400" | A notebook passing a restart-and-run-all check in continuous integration before being archived.>

Cross-reference <ref @fig:nb-pipeline> resolves with a chapter prefix — it is the
first figure of the second chapter — while a reference back to <ref @fig:vcs-graph>
still resolves to the first chapter's figure: the prefix identifies the target's
chapter, not the citing one. The invariant <ref @eqn:nb-invariant> carries the same
chapter prefix.

<chapter | Data Provenance and Lineage>
<author | Sofia Marchetti>
<definition #def:provenance | The *provenance* of a data artifact is the complete record of the inputs and transformations that produced it — enough to recreate the artifact from its raw sources.>

Provenance turns a result into a reproducible one by making its derivation explicit.
<ref @def:provenance> is deliberately strong: a partial record that omits a
transformation is not provenance, because the artifact cannot be rebuilt from it. The
recommended practice, following <cite @Sandve2013>, is to capture provenance
automatically rather than by hand.

<mermaid #fig:lineage caption="A data-lineage graph: raw sources flow through named transformations to derived artifacts.">
graph LR
  R[Raw survey] --> C[Clean]
  C --> M[Merge with registry]
  M --> A[Analysis table]
  A --> P[Published figure]
</mermaid>

A minimal provenance record need capture only three fields per artifact, summarised
in <ref @tab:provenance>.

<csv #tab:provenance caption="The minimal provenance record for a derived artifact.">
field,meaning,example
inputs,identifiers of the source artifacts,raw-survey@a1b2c3
transform,the named and versioned step,clean.py@v2
output,identifier of the produced artifact,analysis-table@d4e5f6
</csv>

The lineage graph in <ref @fig:lineage> is the transitive closure of these
records.<note | In practice the record is emitted by the pipeline tool itself, so
that provenance cannot drift out of sync with the computation it describes.>

<appendix | A Reproducibility Checklist>
The contributors agreed on a short checklist, applicable regardless of tooling:

1. every result traces to a version-controlled commit;
2. every figure regenerates from a script, not by hand;
3. every notebook passes restart-and-run-all in CI;
4. every derived artifact carries a provenance record.

A result that satisfies all four can be rebuilt by a stranger.<note | The checklist
is intentionally tool-agnostic; the chapters give one concrete realisation of each
item, but others are possible.>

